#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124310 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32cbf366-20bd-5a52-a14f-83add698bf07" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit at Infinity: Rational Solutions"}] :question/id #uuid "0fc616fa-c901-53ff-acab-cdada6bb5ce2" :question/math-academy-id "e-80" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\infty} \\dfrac{3x^4+9x}{6x^4-x^3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x^4.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3{x}^{4}+9x}{6{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{9x}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{6{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(3+\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}})}{(6-\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3+0}{6-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124312 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "020c3de3-bcaf-5ade-ba50-2f15e344157d" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit at Infinity: Infinite Solutions"}] :question/id #uuid "b648ed80-e2e1-57c4-bd37-147d92315e90" :question/math-academy-id "e-81" :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow -\\infty} \\dfrac{4-5x-2x^3}{x^2+3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x^2.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{4-5x-2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}+3} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{-2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{5x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(-2x-\\frac{5}{x}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})}{(1+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{-2x+0+0}{1+0}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-2x) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124313 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee15db10-b92c-5f48-8983-e602068c7e08" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Asymptote of a Rational Function"}] :question/id #uuid "c3798fe4-058f-5970-a903-65c654abb788" :question/math-academy-id "e-82" :question/problem "What is the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ with $f(x)=\\dfrac{2x^3+1}{x^3-15x}?$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x^3.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{3}+1}{{x}^{3}-15x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{15x}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(2+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(1-\\frac{15}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+0}{1-0} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124326 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bde258b7-1654-5e73-8703-0e42371db8f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying When a Rational Function has No Horizontal Asymptote"}] :question/id #uuid "32a8e4bb-4191-55a8-883b-baa36f67b061" :question/math-academy-id "e-83" :question/problem "Find the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\dfrac{x^5-10}{x^3-8x^2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x^3.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{5}-10}{{x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{10}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{8{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{({x}^{2}-\\frac{10}{{x}^{3}})}{(1-\\frac{8}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{x}^{2}-0}{1-0}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340997 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bde258b7-1654-5e73-8703-0e42371db8f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying When a Rational Function has No Horizontal Asymptote"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57c1e2df-8bcc-5011-9db9-11fe9a6c4695" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340999} {:answer/id #uuid "5ec15b0d-5160-5486-9468-9ac72d0d9094" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341000} {:answer/id #uuid "0ec79a22-6eef-524b-a114-f45466b6c116" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341001} {:answer/id #uuid "9881eff1-19d5-5d12-bf10-b8cf9d59edb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341002} {:answer/id #uuid "9c15ce9b-efe1-5ee8-8f34-4f3b2207e155" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341003}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ec15b0d-5160-5486-9468-9ac72d0d9094" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341000} :answer-field/id #uuid "03860e69-abc0-52cb-82c9-f3068c82c0b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340998}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7e63b016-2ed8-5744-85e3-ba7a5b9b598c" :question/math-academy-id "q-101319" :question/problem "Find the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+x-1}{3x+2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{2}+x-1}{3x+2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{x}+\\frac{x}{x}-\\frac{1}{x})}{(\\frac{3x}{x}+\\frac{2}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(2x+1-\\frac{1}{x})}{(3+\\frac{2}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(2x+1-0)}{(3+0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2x+1) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341004 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bde258b7-1654-5e73-8703-0e42371db8f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying When a Rational Function has No Horizontal Asymptote"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "825fabcc-dc77-50c1-a74d-517d6520c732" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341006} {:answer/id #uuid "2d44cd0e-f0c7-5440-bc72-34ea3857082a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341007} {:answer/id #uuid "d3db3c90-e72d-5b7b-ac9c-d2cac507767b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341008} {:answer/id #uuid "400914b4-c673-58c8-95b6-7a1a476bb1b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341009} {:answer/id #uuid "cc483799-152e-5118-9756-a2d30022137e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341010}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d44cd0e-f0c7-5440-bc72-34ea3857082a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341007} :answer-field/id #uuid "d33fcbe4-277c-5357-8945-5308d3b4c70e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341005}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c1e689ad-107f-573d-b38b-7f1ffbb249ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-106698" :question/problem "Find the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{7{x}^{5}-3{x}^{2}}{6-2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{7{x}^{5}-3{x}^{2}}{6-2x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{7{x}^{5}}{x}-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{x})}{(\\frac{6}{x}-\\frac{2x}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(7{x}^{4}-3x)}{(\\frac{6}{x}-2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(7{x}^{4}-3x)}{0-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(7{x}^{4}-3x) \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275155 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bde258b7-1654-5e73-8703-0e42371db8f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying When a Rational Function has No Horizontal Asymptote"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d289c4d-66f9-5bc3-ba56-6f40a3dd4628" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275157} {:answer/id #uuid "57140c10-c243-59a6-a36d-d4ee1187cf49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275158} {:answer/id #uuid "eec3b875-66a9-5dff-878d-62bcc4f5aad1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275159} {:answer/id #uuid "938099ab-6674-5275-bf78-41bf3fd9fe1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275160} {:answer/id #uuid "1fd6c2f5-fa61-51d4-8143-2a3ff0cb7e38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275161}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eec3b875-66a9-5dff-878d-62bcc4f5aad1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275159} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bf9256b-03e8-5ac3-88fd-10615dd8ed9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275156}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "062f25d0-e65b-58a6-96b7-d2cc83f47308" :question/math-academy-id "q-106702" :question/problem "Find the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{11{x}^{5}-9{x}^{6}}{2{x}^{5}-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{5}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{11{x}^{5}-9{x}^{6}}{2{x}^{5}-1} \\\\  & {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{11{x}^{5}}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{9{x}^{6}}{{x}^{5}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{5}}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(11-9x)}{(2-\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{11-9x}{2-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(11-9x) \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341011 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee15db10-b92c-5f48-8983-e602068c7e08" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Asymptote of a Rational Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87d01e32-49c1-59bc-b6e4-5333dcefcddc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341013}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87d01e32-49c1-59bc-b6e4-5333dcefcddc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341013} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a793526-c408-5e79-a943-5c9ae00c391c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341012}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9f9b3b79-6712-5d2b-9420-7d4b4caf5430" :question/math-academy-id "q-302242" :question/problem "Given that the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{18{x}^{4}+5x}{c{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}}$ is $y=\\frac{3}{2},$ find the value of $c.$\n\n$c=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{4}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{18{x}^{4}+5x}{c{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{18{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{5x}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{c{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(18+\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}})}{(c-\\frac{2}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{18+0}{c-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{c}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{18}{c} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 36 & =3c \\\\ c & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341014 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32cbf366-20bd-5a52-a14f-83add698bf07" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit at Infinity: Rational Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df03a4ca-7222-5b90-ab10-7896e65d7dd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341016} {:answer/id #uuid "fcbd9826-5bdd-5cd8-a2f1-e2ccfd185fab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341017}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcbd9826-5bdd-5cd8-a2f1-e2ccfd185fab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341017} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa9fc928-6a48-5a69-891b-6361fb00fd55" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341015}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "19f08822-38c8-5644-ae87-60cc80530130" :question/math-academy-id "q-339426" :question/problem "Given that ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3-x-8{x}^{2}}{k\\,{x}^{2}+7x-4}=\\frac{2}{5},$  what is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3-x-8{x}^{2}}{k\\,{x}^{2}+7x-4} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{8{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{k{x}^{2}}{\\,{x}^{2}}+\\frac{7x}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{x}-8)}{(k+\\frac{7}{x}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-0-8}{k+0-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8}{k} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{8}{k} & =\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 2k & =-40 \\\\ k & =-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275168 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "020c3de3-bcaf-5ade-ba50-2f15e344157d" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit at Infinity: Infinite Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74885b07-6eb9-512f-88de-44f3f84c5a50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275170}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74885b07-6eb9-512f-88de-44f3f84c5a50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275170} :answer-field/id #uuid "59067eab-dbc9-5efb-a806-44544690b377" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4c34786e-7329-5ee5-938e-61e317be395c" :question/math-academy-id "q-339612" :question/problem "Evaluate the limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-{x}^{6}+4x}{{x}^{2}+x+5}=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-{x}^{6}+4x}{{x}^{2}+5} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{-{x}^{6}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{4x}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(-{x}^{4}+\\frac{4}{x})}{(1+\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(-{x}^{4}+0)}{(1+0)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,-{x}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275174 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "020c3de3-bcaf-5ade-ba50-2f15e344157d" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit at Infinity: Infinite Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ba531cc-1057-551f-9a86-2fc85ecd71b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275176} {:answer/id #uuid "1336ebc1-00e8-5060-99c4-9d61efe3b6fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275177} {:answer/id #uuid "2a276290-ab0d-52b1-8807-4a9062f36d49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341018}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ba531cc-1057-551f-9a86-2fc85ecd71b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275176} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbf039c6-d675-57a2-b9d8-6e8d13947e79" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275175}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a616dfb2-527b-5098-b8ce-2796ed00b6b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-339623" :question/problem "Evaluate the limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{4}-2}{5{x}^{2}-1}=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{4}-2}{5{x}^{2}-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{({x}^{2}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}{(5-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}-0}{5-0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124827 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee15db10-b92c-5f48-8983-e602068c7e08" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Asymptote of a Rational Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b092275-0441-51f6-a010-037dfba6f9f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121985} {:answer/id #uuid "97fada48-c296-548c-b940-338cfffbd117" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121986} {:answer/id #uuid "e2569ab0-02c7-5ac5-b846-3a3d0cca1aa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121987} {:answer/id #uuid "01ab5ced-488e-53de-a635-3fb03079cdc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121988} {:answer/id #uuid "7f242d0b-0fd1-5380-b194-6c29749e0d9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121989}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01ab5ced-488e-53de-a635-3fb03079cdc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121988} :answer-field/id #uuid "17b4b97e-25e9-5c1e-b9ae-450448f2ae46" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121984}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "24c1ddd3-5086-5a99-bc61-14c84e2545e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-35763" :question/problem "Find the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2x+5}{2{x}^{3}+2x+6}.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2x+5}{2{x}^{3}+2x+6} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}})}{(2+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+0}{2+0+0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ is $y=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341019 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee15db10-b92c-5f48-8983-e602068c7e08" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Asymptote of a Rational Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dcc0c635-a4a1-5f47-adea-6dd1a54780c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341021} {:answer/id #uuid "fc18ba81-9a18-50c6-bd73-8a69f761e55d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341022} {:answer/id #uuid "1a058134-38f6-565d-b2a3-4a284546cca6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341023} {:answer/id #uuid "54725c8f-1119-598a-a096-27128790e6c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341024} {:answer/id #uuid "01612fcc-c163-55eb-bd5c-be51e0fde1d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341025}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcc0c635-a4a1-5f47-adea-6dd1a54780c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341021} :answer-field/id #uuid "af43eb97-41d2-55c2-86db-fccc974e1e51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341020}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dfebc30a-ea33-5139-a1fd-71dc23f9282b" :question/math-academy-id "q-35829" :question/problem "Given that the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{5{x}^{2}-6}{c{x}^{2}+32x+8}$ is $y=\\frac{1}{4},$ find the value of $c.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5{x}^{2}-6}{c{x}^{2}+32x+8} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{c{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{32x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(5-\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}})}{(c+\\frac{32}{x}+\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-0}{c+0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{c}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{c} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ c & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341026 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee15db10-b92c-5f48-8983-e602068c7e08" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Asymptote of a Rational Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4409a5c3-5e66-534b-81cf-a45d267f8f33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341028} {:answer/id #uuid "80e9758c-9a9d-501e-acdd-8edae6497aa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341029} {:answer/id #uuid "1a60afa0-87a8-563c-947c-81930061bf2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341030} {:answer/id #uuid "0db9517c-3b12-5885-be39-a3d9a058c17a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341031} {:answer/id #uuid "13c6a24d-e3e2-578f-8e6e-f957fd31070c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341032}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4409a5c3-5e66-534b-81cf-a45d267f8f33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341028} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc8d34a6-4776-5b40-b346-006e20b21505" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341027}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "64dd18d8-103a-5d01-8ec8-0d7d40e61a5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-35836" :question/problem "Given that the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{24{x}^{3}-1}{2c{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}}$ is $y=-\\frac{1}{3},$ find the value of $c.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{24{x}^{3}-1}{2c{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{24{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2c{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{15{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(24-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(2c-\\frac{15}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{24-0}{2c-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{c}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12}{c} & =-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ c & =-36.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125005 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "020c3de3-bcaf-5ade-ba50-2f15e344157d" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit at Infinity: Infinite Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cc15ea9-a192-5001-abcb-